# tests/assertions/start_pinned_startpos.rxt — [OPT-5] STEP 2's SEEDED
# control for the nonzero-startpos population.
#
# `tests/base/start_pinned_startpos.rxt` carries the pinned patterns; this is
# the one block that could not live there, because `\b` needs module
# `assertions`. It belongs to the same argument and is named in that file's
# header.
#
# WHAT IT CONTROLS. `\bx*` is a machine the start-pinned predicate DECLINES
# (its start state's accept varies with the upcoming byte, so P2 refuses it),
# and it is also a SEEDED machine: a search at `startpos > 0` enters through
# `s1u[]` rather than through `s0`, chosen by the class of the byte BEFORE the
# start position. So it is the row that says a nonzero startpos is reported
# correctly on the DECLINED side of axis J, on the one shape where the entry
# state itself depends on the startpos.
#
# Expectations oracle-verified against python3 `re` (`re.compile(p).search(s,
# pos)`, which is startpos semantics exactly), generated 2026-09-02.

pattern \bx*
features assertions
ns 0 ""
ms 0 "x" 0 1
ms 1 "x" 1 1
ms 0 "ax" 0 0
ms 1 "ax" 2 2
ms 2 "ax" 2 2
ms 0 "a x" 0 0
ms 1 "a x" 1 1
ms 2 "a x" 2 3
ms 3 "a x" 3 3
ms 0 "xx yy" 0 2
ms 1 "xx yy" 2 2
ms 2 "xx yy" 2 2
ms 3 "xx yy" 3 3
ms 4 "xx yy" 5 5
ms 5 "xx yy" 5 5
